<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.2.7: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00068</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.2.7: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>155caacb-c35a-461b-859d-dac709bfef40</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>To help students synthesize their work in the lesson, consider asking them to write a response to one or both of the following prompts:</para>
  <list>
    <item>We could use numbers or letters to represent the quantities in a situation. When might it make sense to use only numbers? When might it make sense to use letters?</item>
    <item>You’ve heard the phrases “a quantity that varies” and “a quantity that stays constant” in this lesson. Describe what they mean in your own words. If possible, give an example of a situation that has a quantity that varies and a quantity that stays constant.</item>
  </list>
</section>
</content>
</document>
